<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amoghavarsha I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–<a href="/years/878">878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amoghavarsha I was the longest-reigning Rashtrakuta emperor, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from 814 to 878. He founded the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>, was a patron of Jain teachers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a>, and is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavirajamarga">Kavirajamarga</a>, the earliest surviving work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poetics, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> Prashnottara Ratnamalika. His long reign was marked more by cultural flowering than by conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i.jpg" alt="Amoghavarsha I" width="320" height="551" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B. Lewis Rice (Benjamin Lewis Rice, 1837-1927) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_(876_AD)_of_Rashtrakuta_King_Amoghavarsha_I_at_Veerabhadra_temple_in_Kumsi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amoghavarsha, whose birth name was Sharva or Shravan, was born in 800 at Sribhavan on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>, while his father, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-iii">Govinda III</a>, was returning from his campaigns in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Manne inscription of 803 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a> plates of 871 are the chief sources for his life. He came to the throne at the age of 14, after his father's death, and took titles such as Nrupatunga, Atishadhavala, Veeranarayana, Rattamarthanda and Srivallabha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign a revolt of relatives and feudatories briefly unseated him. The Western Ganga king Shivamara II rose first and was killed in 816, but Amoghavarsha's commander Bankesha was beaten at Rajaramadu by the next Ganga king, Rachamalla, and the emperor turned to conciliation, marrying his daughters Chandrabbalabbe to Butuga I and Revakanimmadi to prince Ereganga. With the help of his cousin Karka, also called Patamalla, he had overcome all resistance by 821.</p><p>In the east the Eastern Chalukya Vijayaditya II had seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> from the Rashtrakuta feudatory Bhima Salki. Amoghavarsha defeated the Vengi Chalukyas at the battle of Vingavalli, and he killed Vishnuvardhana V in 846 after that king attacked the Kalachuri feudatory at Tripuri. He kept friendly ties with the next Eastern Chalukya ruler, Gunaga Vijayaditya III, and with the Pallavas, and in 870 he put down the Alupas of South Canara. He moved the capital from Mayurkhandi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>. Towards the end of his life he is said to have given up the throne voluntarily.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Amoghavarsha was a devout Jain, a disciple and patron of the Digambar teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a>, and he built Jain temples at Ellora; the Jain Narayana temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a> and the Neminatha Basadi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a> date from his reign. He also supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, including a donation to the Mahalakshmi Temple at Kolhapur, and let Muslim merchants trade in peace.</p><p>The mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahaviracharya">Mahaviracharya</a> dedicated his Ganita-sara-samgraha to a Rashtrakuta king often identified with him. The Arab traveller Sulaiman al-Tajir counted him among the four great kings of the world, and the historian R. S. Panchamukhi called him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> of the South. The Prashnottara Ratnamalika is said to date from his old age, though some attribute it to Vimalacharya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amoghavarsha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Amoghavarsha I</b> the Jain teachers Jinasena and Gunabhadra composed the Adipurana, and the mathematician Mahaviracharya wrote the Ganitasarasangraha c. 850.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
